{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_12","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"find /home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed1425705205/3 -maxdepth 2 -type f | sort | xargs -r -I{} sh -lc 'printf \\\"FILE %s\\\\n\\\" \\\"\"'$1\"; sed -n \"1,220p\" \"$1\"'\"' _ {}\"","aggregated_output":"FILE /home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed1425705205/3/des.md\n<new_puzzle>\n- `description`：根据边框形状内的\"种子花纹\"递归生长出分支结构，分支方向由邻接的色块指示。\n\n- `Input structure`：\n  - 背景为 black。网格中散布着若干由单一颜色绘制的小型封闭矩形边框（宽高 3-5 格，边框线宽 1 格），每个边框内部包含一个 \"种子花纹\"——由另一种颜色绘制的 1-3 个像素组成的小图案。\n  - 每个边框的外侧四条边上，恰好有一条或多条边的中点处紧贴着一个单像素色块（第三种颜色），称为\"芽点\"。\n  - 芽点的颜色在整个网格中统一，但芽点的数量和位置因边框而异。\n\n- `Rule`：\n  - 第一步——花纹方向映射：观察种子花纹在边框内部的朝向。若花纹的非对称像素偏向边框的某一侧（上/下/左/右），则该侧被定义为花纹的\"头部方向\"。\n  - 第二步——分支生长：从每个芽点出发，沿芽点所在边的法线方向（朝外）生长出一条直线\"枝干\"。枝干的长度等于种子花纹中非背景像素的数量。枝干颜色与边框颜色相同。\n  - 第三步——枝端复制：在每条枝干的末端，复制一份种子花纹。复制时需要旋转花纹，使花纹的头部方向与枝干的生长方向一致。复制的花纹使用原花纹颜色。\n  - 边框本身和原始种子花纹保持不变。超出网格边界的部分截断不绘制。\n\n- `Key insight`：解题者需要先从花纹的非对称形状中辨认出\"头部方向\"这个隐含属性，再理解芽点触发定长枝干生长并在末端旋转复制花纹的两步机制——花纹既是\"被复制的模板\"又是\"编码枝干长度的计数器\"。\n</new_puzzle>\n\n<new_puzzle>\n- `description`：将散落的彩色碎片按颜色归入对应的\"信封\"框架中，碎片的放置位置由信封上的窗口缺口决定。\n\n- `Input structure`：\n  - 背景为 black。网格中有若干个用不同颜色绘制的矩形空心框架（边框宽 1 格），称为\"信封\"。每个信封的某一条边上有一个 1 格宽的缺口（即该边有一格被擦除为背景色）。\n  - 每个信封内部为空（全黑）。信封之间互不重叠、互不相邻。\n  - 网格中还散布着若干小型彩色精灵（sprite），每个精灵是一个 4-连通的单色块，颜色各不相同。\n  - 关键约束：每个精灵的颜色恰好与某一个信封的边框颜色相同。精灵的宽度或高度恰好等于 1 格（即精灵是一条水平或竖直的线段）。\n\n- `Rule`：\n  - 第一步——颜色配对：将每个精灵与同色的信封配对。\n  - 第二步——缺口定位：找到每个信封的缺口位置。缺口所在的边决定了\"插入方向\"——从缺口向信封内部的方向即为插入方向。\n  - 第三步——滑入放置：将精灵从缺口处沿插入方向\"滑入\"信封内部。精灵的长轴平行于缺口所在的边，居中对齐缺口位置。精灵沿插入方向滑到信封内壁对面的边为止（即紧贴对面内壁放置）。\n  - 第四步——缺口封闭：将缺口处的像素用信封颜色填上，使信封变为完整的封闭矩形。\n  - 原始精灵从网格中移除（原位置变为 black）。\n\n- `Key insight`：解题者需要先发现颜色配对关系，再识别缺口的位置和朝向来确定插入方向，最后理解精灵是\"穿过缺口滑到对面内壁\"的运动语义——缺口既是入口也是最终被封闭的修补点。\n</new_puzzle>\n\n<new_puzzle>\n- `description`：在分区网格中，根据每个区域内\"指针\"图案所指的邻区来传递并叠加颜色层。\n\n- `Input structure`：\n  - 背景为 black。网格被 grey 色的单像素宽分隔线划分为 N×M 个大小相同的矩形区域（类似表格）。\n  - 每个区域内包含两类元素：\n    1. 一个\"指针\"——由 maroon 色像素组成的箭头形状，箭头由 3 个像素构成：一个像素作为箭尾，相邻的一个像素作为箭身，再沿同方向相邻的一个像素作为箭头。箭头方向指向上/下/左/右四个方向之一。\n    2. 零个或多个\"载荷像素\"——非 black、非 grey、非 maroon 的彩色像素，散布在区域内。\n\n- `Rule`：\n  - 第一步——确定传递链：每个区域的指针指向一个相邻区域（上/下/左/右）。如果指针指向网格外部（边缘区域指向边界外），则该区域为\"终端区域\"，不向外传递。\n  - 第二步——收集载荷：每个区域收集自身的载荷像素，以及所有通过传递链最终指向本区域的其他区域的载荷像素。传递链可以多级串联（A 指向 B，B 指向 C，则 A 的载荷最终传递到 C）。\n  - 第三步——输出绘制：输出网格去掉所有分隔线和指针。每个区域保持原始大小，其中显示该区域最终收集到的所有载荷像素。收集来的外部载荷像素按原始相对位置（相对于来源区域左上角的偏移）放置到目标区域中。若多个像素落在同一位置，保留颜色编号更大的那个（即后来叠加的覆盖先前的，使用颜色在调色板中的顺序：black < blue < red < green < yellow < grey < magenta < orange < azure < maroon）。\n\n- `Key insight`：解题者需要先从箭头方向构建出完整的区域间传递链（可能形成多级串联），再理解载荷像素如何以相对坐标叠加到最终目标区域中——指针网络的全局拓扑而非单个箭头的局部方向才是关键。\n</new_puzzle>\n\n<new_puzzle>\n- `description`：根据连通区域的拓扑嵌套关系构建层级树，然后按照树的深度对每层区域重新着色。\n\n- `Input structure`：\n  - 背景为 black。网格中有多个由不同颜色绘制的封闭环形区域（每个区域是一个 4-连通的单色环，环的内部为 black 或包含更小的环）。\n  - 这些环形成嵌套结构：最外层的大环包围着中层环，中层环又包围着内层环，类似同心环但形状不规则。嵌套层数从 1 到 4 不等。\n  - 输入中各环的颜色是随机分配的，与嵌套层级无关。\n  - 网格中还有一个独立的小区域作为\"调色板\"——由上到下排列的 K 个单像素色块（K 等于最大嵌套深度），每个像素颜色不同，形成一个有序颜色序列。\n\n- `Rule`：\n  - 第一步——构建嵌套树：将所有封闭环按包含关系组织成树结构。最外层环（直接被 black 背景包围的）为深度 1，被深度 1 的环包围的环为深度 2，以此类推。\n  - 第二步——调色板映射：调色板从上到下第 1 个颜色对应深度 1，第 2 个颜色对应深度 2，依次类推。\n  - 第三步——重新着色：将每个环的颜色替换为其深度对应的调色板颜色。环内未被更深层环占据的 black 区域保持 black。\n  - 调色板区域在输出中移除（变为 black）。\n\n- `Key insight`：解题者需要先识别出环的拓扑嵌套关系（而非简单的空间位置或大小排序），再发现调色板的顺序编码了深度到颜色的映射——核心难点在于理解\"包含关系\"定义了层级，而不是面积大小或位置远近。\n</new_puzzle>\n\n<new_puzzle>\n- `description`：在网格中找到多个残缺的周期图案块，利用它们互相补全缺失部分，输出修复后的完整图案。\n\n- `Input structure`：\n  - 背景为 black。网格中有 2-4 个相同大小的矩形区域，彼此不重叠，由背景间隔分开。\n  - 每个矩形区域内包含同一个周期性彩色图案（使用 2-3 种非背景色）的一部分，但每个区域都有若干像素被\"损坏\"——替换为 grey 色。\n  - 不同区域的损坏位置不同，但底层的完整图案相同。也就是说，区域 A 中被 grey 覆盖的位置，在区域 B 或 C 中可能是完好的（显示原色）。\n  - 所有区域合在一起，图案的每个位置至少有一个区域是未损坏的。\n\n- `Rule`：\n  - 第一步——对齐识别：解题者需要识别出所有矩形区域包含的是同一个图案的不同\"损坏版本\"。\n  - 第二步——交叉修复：对于每个区域中被 grey 覆盖的像素，从其他区域的同一相对位置找到未损坏的原色，用该颜色替换 grey。\n  - 第三步——输出：输出仅包含一个修复后的完整图案矩形（取第一个区域的位置和大小），所有 grey 像素都已被还原为正确颜色。\n\n- `Key insight`：表面上看每个区域是独立的损坏图案，解题者需要发现它们其实是同一图案的互补副本——grey 是统一的\"损坏标记\"，而修复的关键在于跨区域的对应位置参照，而非在单个区域内通过周期性推断缺失值。\n</new_puzzle>\n\n<new_puzzle>\n- `description`：根据\"路标\"像素指示的方向在迷宫通道中传播染色波，波的颜色由起点区域决定，交汇处按混色规则叠加。\n\n- `Input structure`：\n  - 背景为 black。网格中有 green 色像素绘制的单像素宽通道网络，形成若干条互相交叉的水平和竖直走廊。\n  - 通道的某些交叉点处放置了\"路标\"——一个 orange 色像素，表示该交叉点是分流节点。\n  - 通道的若干死端（只有一个方向连通的端点）各放置了一个彩色\"染料源\"像素（使用 red、blue、yellow 三种颜色之一），替代该位置原本的 green 像素。\n\n- `Rule`：\n  - 第一步——波的发射：每个染料源沿其唯一连通方向向通道中发射一道\"染色波\"。波沿通道前进，将沿途的 green 像素替换为染料源的颜色。\n  - 第二步——路标分流：当波到达一个 orange 路标时，波向该交叉点所有连通方向（除了波的来源方向）继续传播。路标像素本身被替换为波的颜色。\n  - 第三步——波的终止：波在到达通道死端、网格边界或遇到已被染色（非 green、非 orange）的像素时停止。\n  - 第四步——颜色叠加：如果两道不同颜色的波同时到达同一个像素（即它们沿通道的传播距离相等），该像素变为 maroon。如果一道波先到达，则保留先到波的颜色，后到的波在此处停止。\n  - 传播距离按沿通道经过的像素数计算（即路径长度）。\n\n- `Key insight`：解题者需要先理解通道拓扑和路标的分流作用，再追踪多个波源沿通道的同步传播过程——关键难点在于判断不同波到达同一位置的先后顺序取决于沿通道的路径长度，而交汇处的混色规则提供了验证线索。\n</new_puzzle>\nFILE /home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed1425705205/3/ids.txt\neval/b0039139\neval/195c6913\neval/3a25b0d8\neval/135a2760\neval/3dc255db\neval/a32d8b75\neval/6ffbe589\neval/7ed72f31\neval/cbebaa4b\neval/9aaea919\neval/da515329\neval/65b59efc\neval/aa4ec2a5\neval/981571dc\neval/b99e7126\neval/c4d067a0\neval/e87109e9\neval/5dbc8537\neval/898e7135\neval/89565ca0\nFILE /home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed1425705205/3/prompt.md\n你是一个 ARC-AGI 谜题设计专家。下面的 20 道参考题是难度和机制质量的标杆。仔细阅读每道题的**解题逻辑**（解题者从样例归纳规则的认知过程），而非表面场景。你的新题应在机制深度和隐蔽性上与参考题持平或者实现超越。\n\n每个参考题描述包含：\n- `description`：一句话概述题目的变换目标。\n- `Input structure`：输入网格中有哪些对象、背景、标记、边界或组件。\n- `Rule`：从输入到输出的确定性变换规则。\n- `Key insight`：解题所需的核心洞见。\n\n参考题目如下：\n<reference_puzzle index=\"1\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"b0039139\" lines=\"29-44\">\n<description>\nRecolor a cropped template and repeat it according to a component-count stencil.\n\nInput structure:\n- Solid blue (1) separator rows or columns split the grid into four sections.\n- Section 1 is a black-background shape template; section 2 is a component-count stencil.\n- Sections 3 and 4 are solid color swatches for output foreground and background.\n\nRule:\n- Crop section 1 to the bbox of its non-black pixels and recolor it with the two swatches.\n- Count 4-connected non-black components in section 2.\n- Repeat the recolored crop that many times, inserting one background-colored row/column between copies.\n\nKey insight: The second section encodes only the repeat count; the output orientation\nis inherited from whether the input separators are columns or rows.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"2\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"195c6913\" lines=\"37-52\">\n<description>\nSimulate particles along a path with alternating turn direction at collisions.\n\nInput structure:\n- Top-left: 2x2 monochrome blocks define a repeating color cycle (left-to-right).\n- Isolated single pixels of a cycle color: start markers adjacent to the path.\n- A 2x2 monochrome block of a distinct color: end marker.\n\nRule:\n- Each start marker launches a particle moving straight, painting cycle colors in order.\n- On hitting a boundary, paint that cell with the end color and turn 90°.\n- Turn direction alternates: odd collisions → left (CCW), even → right (CW).\n- If the scheduled turn is blocked, the particle stops.\n\nKey insight: The alternating left/right turn rule is the core mechanism; without it the path would be trivial.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"3\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"3a25b0d8\" lines=\"9-22\">\n<description>\nFill holes in a target object with colors from a source object's subsprites, then tight-crop.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = background. Two similar 8-connected objects share an outline color.\n- Source has colored subsprites in cavities; target has empty cavities.\n\nRule:\n- Find the two 8-connected objects.\n- Target = object with more hole area. Align subsprites to holes (rotation/flip if needed).\n- Fill each hole with its matched subsprite color; tight-crop.\n\nKey insight: Hole centers in the target are matched to source subsprite centers under the best rotation/flip transform.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"4\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"135a2760\" lines=\"6-19\">\n<description>\nRepair noisy periodic patterns inside frame-delimited grid regions.\n\nInput structure:\n- Frame color (most common) forms full rows/cols, dividing the grid into rectangular regions.\n- bg (second most common) fills empty space; each region contains a small colored tile repeated periodically with a few corrupted pixels.\n\nRule:\n- For each region, find the bounding box of non-bg content.\n- Infer the smallest repeating tile that explains the pattern (majority vote per residue position), requiring it repeat at least 2x per axis.\n- Replace the region with the clean tiled version.\n\nKey insight: Tile dimensions are unknown and must be inferred; prefer the smallest tile with genuine repetition over one that memorizes the entire region.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"5\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"3dc255db\" lines=\"37-53\">\n<description>\nEject content pixels from cup-shaped frames outward along each cup's tail direction.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = background. Hollow cup/Y/funnel frames, each a unique border color.\n- Each cup has a convergent tail spike on one side and an open mouth on the opposite side.\n- Content pixels (a single non-border color per cup) sit inside the cup's mouth interior.\n\nRule:\n- For each cup, identify its tail spike and tail direction.\n- Erase all content pixels from the mouth interior.\n- Draw a straight line of content-colored pixels from the spike outward in the tail direction,\n  one pixel per erased content pixel; cells beyond the grid edge are dropped.\n\nKey insight: The tail spike is the unique convergent point whose outward direction is opposite\nthe mouth opening where the content lives.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"6\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"a32d8b75\" lines=\"262-275\">\n<description>\nAssemble and place enlarged sprites on a canvas using legend-panel instructions.\n\nInput structure:\n- Color-6 separators divide the grid into a left legend column and a main canvas.\n- Each legend panel has (top to bottom): a unit sprite, a frame template, a 5x5 bordered square with a position-marker pixel, and a 3x3 rotation clock-hand.\n\nRule:\n- Tile the unit sprite into each active cell of the frame to build an enlarged composite.\n- Rotate the composite per the clock-hand direction (up=0, right=90, down=180, left=270 degrees).\n- Place it on the canvas anchored at the position encoded by the marker pixel's row/col in the bordered square's 3x3 inner grid.\n\nKey insight: The marker pixel's position in a 3x3 grid jointly encodes vertical and horizontal anchor alignment (start/middle/end).\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"7\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"6ffbe589\" lines=\"83-98\">\n<description>\nCrop a board cluster and rotate its color layers based on outside cue sprites.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color.\n- A main multi-color board cluster (largest foreground region).\n- Small monochrome cue sprites outside the board for the colors that rotate.\n\nRule:\n- Tight-crop around the board cluster.\n- For each color layer inside the crop, find its same-color cue outside.\n- Rotate that layer clockwise by (cue pixel count mod 4) × 90°.\n- No outside cue → no rotation.\n\nKey insight: Cue sprite size mod 4 encodes rotation amount for its matching color layer.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"8\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"7ed72f31\" lines=\"8-22\">\n<description>\nMirror colored sprites using red (2) markers as reflection axes.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color. Red (2) = marker color.\n- Colored sprites appear near red markers.\n\nRule:\n- Red line within a sprite (vertical or horizontal) → mirror payload across that axis.\n- Single red pixel in a mixed sprite → point-reflect payload around it.\n- Isolated red pixel (alone, no payload) → point-reflect each adjacent 8-connected color component.\n- Originals remain; mirrored pixels overlay.\n\nKey insight: Red marker geometry (line vs single pixel) determines reflection type; isolated vs embedded red determines what gets reflected.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"9\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"cbebaa4b\" lines=\"51-65\">\n<description>\nAssemble scattered jigsaw sprites around a central yellow anchor by pairing red connector pixels.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = black (0). Several colored sprites scattered on the grid.\n- One anchor sprite contains a 3×3 yellow (4) block.\n- Each sprite has red (2) connector pixels along its edges.\n\nRule:\n- The anchor stays in place; remaining sprites attach one at a time.\n- Each sprite is translated so that one of its red pixels overlaps an unpaired red pixel on the growing assembly.\n- Every red connector must pair with exactly one other, forming a complete joined structure.\n\nKey insight: Red pixels act as jigsaw pegs — each must match exactly one counterpart, and the assembly order is determined by which unpaired connectors remain.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"10\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"9aaea919\" lines=\"38-51\">\n<description>\nTransfer sprites from red-marked lanes to green-marked lanes, recoloring and stacking.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color. Red (2) and green (3) thin bars define vertical lanes.\n- Each lane contains repeated identical monochrome sprites in an evenly spaced vertical grid.\n\nRule:\n- Each red lane's sprites turn grey (5); their count transfers to the nearest green lane.\n- New sprites fill empty lattice slots bottom-up, using the source shape recolored to the destination lane's sprite color.\n- All marker bars are removed.\n\nKey insight: Sprite count is conserved across lanes — red sprites become grey, and equally many new sprites stack into the green lane's regular grid.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"11\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"da515329\" lines=\"11-24\">\n<description>\nGrow four clockwise spiral wall paths from the tips of a teal cross.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = 0 (black). A single teal (8) cross centered in the grid.\n\nRule:\n- Each arm tip launches a \"turtle\" heading 90° right of its outward direction.\n- Movement priority: turn right > go straight > turn left (left only if not on border).\n- Normal step = 2 cells; step right after a turn = 1 cell.\n- Each new cell must have a 1-cell orthogonal gap from all existing walls except the predecessor.\n\nKey insight: The right-turn preference plus alternating 2/1 step lengths creates self-avoiding spirals that tile the grid with uniform spacing.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"12\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"65b59efc\" lines=\"9-24\">\n<description>\nDecode a stamp map: read templates, markers, and palette from a tiled grid, then render stamps.\n\nInput structure:\n- Grid divided into K tile columns (each N wide) separated by 1-col dashed separators.\n- Top band (rows 0..N-1): K template masks, one per tile column.\n- Middle band (rows N+1..2N): marker pixels whose color picks a template.\n- Bottom band (rows 2N+2..): K palette swatches giving the output color per template.\n- Separator rows at rows N and 2N+1 (dashed pattern of sep color).\n\nRule:\n- Each marker's local position (lr, lc) within its tile column determines which N×N output block to stamp.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
